    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Goguryeo grew from a clan federation on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu</a> into the largest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-of-korea">Korea's Three Kingdoms</a>, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and the northern peninsula.</li><li>It took the Chinese commandery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelang-commandery">Lelang</a> in 313 and adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, a Confucian academy and a law code under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosurim-of-goguryeo">King Sosurim</a>.</li><li>Its fortresses held off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang invasions for seventy years before a Tang and Silla alliance took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> in 668.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Goguryeo itself was often called Goryeo from the 5th century, and through the later kingdom of Goryeo that name became the English word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

