    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gungnae"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gungnae.jpg" alt="Gungnae" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gungnae">Gungnae</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corner_of_Gungnae_Fortress_(GuoNei_Fortress).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DsouzaSohan, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Goguryeo grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemaek">Yemaek</a> peoples of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu</a> and Hun river valleys, a rugged region of mountains and narrow farmland on the borders of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>. Chinese sources mention a Gaogouli county within the Han commandery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuantu-commandery">Xuantu</a> after 107 BCE, and the Goguryeo polity took shape among these communities as a federation of five clans, or <em>bu</em>. Korean tradition, recorded centuries later in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a></em> of 1145, holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dongmyeong-of-goguryeo">Jumong</a>, a prince who fled from the kingdom of Buyeo, founded the state in 37 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolbon">Jolbon</a>, usually identified with the area of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huanren">Huanren</a> and the mountain fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wunu-mountain">Wunü Shan</a>. The same tradition places the move of the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gungnae">Gungnae</a>, at modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ji-an-jilin">Ji'an</a> on the Yalu, in 3 CE under King Yuri; archaeology confirms Ji'an as the royal centre for the following four centuries. According to the Chinese <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Sanguozhi</a></em>, kingship passed early on from the Sono clan to the Gyeru, whose kings bore the royal surname Go. Under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goguryeo">Taejo</a>, whose traditional reign of 53–146 is implausibly long, Goguryeo subjugated the Okjeo and Dongye peoples to the east and began to raid the Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> and Xuantu.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyongyang.jpg" alt="Pyongyang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Arch_of_Triumph_(11360607534).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Clay Gilliland, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Goguryeo's mountainous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> offered little good farmland, and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Sanguozhi</a></em> of the late 3rd century describes a frugal, warlike people whose great households lived on grain, fish and salt carried to them by commoners and by subject peoples such as the Okjeo. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">Millet</a>, barley and soybeans were the main crops, supplemented by hunting, stock-raising and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-working">iron-working</a>, and the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> plain and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taedong-river">Taedong</a> basin around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> in the 4th and 5th centuries greatly widened the agricultural base. Society was sharply ranked between a hereditary aristocracy of the five <em>bu</em>, free commoners who owed taxes and labour, and dependent or enslaved people taken in war. The same Chinese account records a great state festival, <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dongmaeng">Dongmaeng</a></em>, held in the tenth month to honour Heaven, and a marriage custom in which the groom lived in the bride's family home until a child was grown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, officially received in 372, spread among the elite alongside older ancestral and sky cults, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Daoism</a> was favoured at court in the 7th century. The painted tombs near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ji-an-jilin">Ji'an</a> and Pyongyang, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anak-tomb-no-3">Anak Tomb No. 3</a>, dated 357, and the Tomb of the Dancers, show banquets, kitchens, hunts, wrestlers and armoured cavalry.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-the-great.jpg" alt="Gwanggaeto the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-the-great">Gwanggaeto the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gwanggaeto_Stele_5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EditQ, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Goguryeo was organised for war. Its strength rested on aristocratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, in which both rider and horse were armoured, supported by infantry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and spearmen, and on a dense network of stone-walled mountain fortresses (<em>sanseong</em>) that sheltered troops and population and forced invaders into slow sieges. Setbacks came early: in 244 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanqiu-jian">Guanqiu Jian</a> sacked the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hwando">Hwando</a>, and in 342 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong Xianbei</a> sacked it again, carrying off the queen mother and the body of the previous king. Expansion resumed with the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelang-commandery">Lelang</a> in 313 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daifang-commandery">Daifang</a> soon after, ending four centuries of Chinese commandery rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, although King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gogugwon-of-goguryeo">Gogugwon</a> was killed defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> against Baekje in 371. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-the-great">Gwanggaeto the Great</a> (r. 391–413) campaigned against Baekje, the Later Yan, the Khitan and eastern Buyeo, and in 400 sent an army to relieve Silla from Wa and Gaya attackers. In 475 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jangsu-of-goguryeo">King Jangsu</a> took the Baekje capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanseong</a> and executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaero-of-baekje">King Gaero</a>. Goguryeo's defences met their greatest test in the wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang: Sui Wendi's expedition of 598 failed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Sui Yangdi</a>'s huge army of 612 was routed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eulji-mundeok">Eulji Mundeok</a> at the Salsu River, and in 645 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Tang Taizong</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> fortress but abandoned the siege of Ansi.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jangsu-of-goguryeo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jangsu-of-goguryeo.jpg" alt="Jangsu of Goguryeo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jangsu-of-goguryeo">Jangsu of Goguryeo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_the_General_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bart0278, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Early Goguryeo kings headed a federation, sharing power with the chiefs of the other four <em>bu</em>, who kept their own officials. From the 2nd century the crown gradually turned the <em>bu</em> into divisions of an aristocracy based in the capital, and the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosurim-of-goguryeo">King Sosurim</a> (r. 371–384) laid the foundations of a centralised state: the reception of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> from the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> in 372, the founding that same year of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taehak">Taehak</a></em>, a Confucian academy for aristocratic youths, and the promulgation of a law code (<em>yullyeong</em>) in 373. Officials were graded in a hierarchy of a dozen or more ranks, headed in the later period by a chief minister, the <em>daedaero</em>, who according to Chinese accounts was chosen among the great nobles, at times by force of arms. Provinces were run from the fortresses, whose commanders combined military, fiscal and judicial authority, and Chinese sources describe three capitals: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gungnae">Gungnae</a> and a southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanseong</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-the-great">Gwanggaeto</a>'s stele at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ji-an-jilin">Ji'an</a>, erected by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jangsu-of-goguryeo">Jangsu</a> in 414, is both a royal genealogy and a register of tomb-guard households, showing an administration that kept records of people. In 642 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeon-gaesomun">Yeon Gaesomun</a> killed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeongnyu-of-goguryeo">Yeongnyu</a> and more than a hundred officials, placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bojang-of-goguryeo">Bojang</a> on the throne and ruled as military dictator with the title <em>dae mangniji</em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-stele"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-stele.jpg" alt="Gwanggaeto Stele" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-stele">Gwanggaeto Stele</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20230605_Stela_for_Gwanggaeto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yumeto, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Goguryeo was the dominant power of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> for much of the 4th–6th centuries and an early channel through which Chinese writing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, Confucian learning and law reached the Korean peninsula. Its ascendancy shaped the politics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a>: Silla was for a time a near-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, as the stele and a bronze vessel inscribed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-the-great">Gwanggaeto</a>'s name found in a Silla tomb in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a> attest, while Baekje's long rivalry with Goguryeo drove its alliances with the southern Chinese courts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamato-court">Yamato Japan</a>. Goguryeo monks and artists also travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>; the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyeja">Hyeja</a> taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-shotoku">Prince Shōtoku</a>, and the <em>Nihon shoki</em> credits the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damjing">Damjing</a> with introducing the making of paper and ink in 610. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-stele">Gwanggaeto Stele</a>, rediscovered around 1880, is among the most important and most contested inscriptions in East Asian history, especially for its passages on the Wa. After 668 Goguryeo's people and traditions passed into Silla and into Balhae, founded in 698 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dae-joyeong">Dae Joyeong</a>, which presented itself to Japan as Goguryeo's heir. The painted tombs are listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/complex-of-koguryo-tombs">Complex of Koguryo Tombs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capital-cities-and-tombs-of-the-ancient-koguryo-kingdom">Capital Cities and Tombs of the Ancient Koguryo Kingdom</a>. In recent decades Chinese and Korean historians have disputed whether Goguryeo belongs to Korean or Chinese history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang.jpg" alt="Emperor Taizong of Tang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Emperor Taizong of Tang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TangTaizong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of Goguryeo began with the loss of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> basin, held since 475, to a Silla and Baekje alliance in 551; Silla then seized the whole area in 553 and gained direct access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. At the same time the reunification of China under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> in 589 confronted Goguryeo with a far larger neighbour that would not tolerate its hold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>. Goguryeo survived four Sui invasions between 598 and 614, and the cost of these failures helped bring down the Sui dynasty itself in 618, but the wars drained its manpower. Under the Tang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Taizong</a>'s campaign of 645 and further attacks in the 650s and 660s wore down the frontier fortresses, and Tang's alliance with Silla, which destroyed Baekje in 660, opened a second front in the south. The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeon-gaesomun">Yeon Gaesomun</a> in 665 or 666 set his sons against one another; the eldest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeon-namsaeng">Yeon Namsaeng</a>, defected to the Tang and guided its armies. In 668 the Tang general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-shiji">Li Ji</a>, with Silla forces, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> and captured King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bojang-of-goguryeo">Bojang</a>. The Tang set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate-general-to-pacify-the-east">Protectorate General to Pacify the East</a> there and deported tens of thousands of households to China. Restoration movements followed, including the Silla-sponsored kingdom of Bodeok under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anseung">Anseung</a> (670–684), and many Goguryeo people later joined Balhae.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Goguryeo was the most powerful of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-of-korea">Korea's Three Kingdoms</a>, a fortress-based warrior state that ended Chinese commandery rule in the peninsula, transmitted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and Chinese learning eastward, held off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang for seventy years, and left its name to Goryeo and ultimately to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> itself.</em></p>

